#passage(("My morality comes from the Quran; based on that I give the value to different objects. Now when it comes about God, when you ask me: do you have evidence for God? I will turn the table on you. I would say that it's a human innate thing to believe in a Creator. My question is: where does that belief come from? Why does every human being be born with the same idea of God, something transcendent, beyond? The children have it. You can look at the studies. They believe in something higher, above, unlike the creation. Something individual, or powerful; you don't make it a human being.", "The attributes that they have are limited to Islam. Like, we don't believe that God is a man, so they don't say God is a man. We believe Allah is beyond our comprehension. We believe Allah is All-Powerful, above. They have these main things that Muslims believe about the Creator. My question is: where does this commonality come from? No one is born saying Jesus is God, or Brahma is God. They don't have to say the Name, but they have the description. They say higher power, above, who can not be observed, above our comprehension as human beings — that is what we believe — and created the Universe.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Vegan Gets Emotional! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-6NWYbDw18k", ((224, "3:44–5:10"), (325, "5:25–6:09"),), id: 9)
