#passage(("You made another important statement which was false: you said the existence of these false ideas of God is a testimony to the signature of a human being. That's incorrect, because the reason people believed in a Creator is because they're born believing in a Creator. That Creator that created us, all of us, has put in an innate signature, what some people refer to as a signature of God, that exists in every human being, that is innate.", "Now, where is the evidence for that? There are many evidences for that. There are studies that have been conducted. For instance, Dr. Olivera Petrovich conducted a study specifically on Japanese children, because she had a Japanese colleague and he was telling her: \"We have Shinto religion, we don't have God. God does not exist in our idea, in our culture, in our society.\" Atheist, by the way, all of them. They have no skin in the game to say \"there is a God\"; they don't even believe in a God! But they say that all of these children are born innately believing in a Creator.", "So, there's an innate idea that has been shown, historically, that humans have this innate idea of a divine, something higher than themselves. Now, you'll have certain people ascribing this divinity to a false idea. For instance, they see thunder, they thought: \"OK, there's a god of thunder.\" That does not mean that these people were evil, because when they saw thunder, for instance, they thought that God was angry with them, so they need to do good; that doesn't come from a bad position, from an evil nature of a human being. I'll say the existence of these things is more of a testimony that there is a Creator! Because it just strengthens this idea that it's a natural innate thing in every human being to submit and want to worship something that is higher than themselves. So, I'll say if what you're saying is true, it's actually MORE evidence that there is a Creator than there isn't.", "It's a self-contradictory response. You said that peopl