#passage(("The universe has a starting point, and its starting point is the Big Bang, where you have matter and energy. And we all agree that matter and energy are not conscious being that act on their own. They require a cause by definition. That's why the Big Bang is an effect that requires a cause. They cannot function on their own. They're not a live entity that can make decisions, that has a will. So I can easily ask this question: where does the energy and matter come from, and what causes them? I can ask this rational question due to the fact that these are not conscious entities that behave on their own.", "Now, when we talk about that they must come from something, that something, the creator, whatever it is, you come with the question that is irrational: where does the creator come from? You have two problems there. The main problem is that you're assuming the creator comes from something. We don't believe the creator has a starting point. We don't believe the creator starts coming into existence like the universe starts to come to existence. We don't believe the creator is an entity that is not conscious, that doesn't make decisions, that doesn't have a will. We don't believe any of that. So you're making a false comparison.", "But then I'll also say, okay, let's accept the hypothetical scenario that the creator has a creator. If the creator has a creator, and the creator has a creator, and the creator has a creator, would anything exist? It's a fact. I wouldn't never be able to create until I'm created. And the other thing cannot be created until it's created. And nothing will start. The fact that we exist refutes the existence of an infinite regression of causes in the past. How is it refuted by us being here? Because we wouldn't be here if it's impossible.", "Therefore, there must be something that initiates existence and the creation. That thing we've just proved, God, because it's the eternal thing. So this is what humans, or Muslims, Christians, and