#passage(("That is another problem the Atheists fall into — it's a misunderstanding of language, because right now you said: I like potato, but I don't like potato, in the same time. When you say \"cause\", the definition of cause is bringing something into existence. The definition of \"create\" is bringing something into existence. So when you say \"cause\" and not \"create\", you're just conflating terms here in a way. So you're just essentially saying the thing and refuting it at the same time. So if you agree that there's a cause for the universe, you agree by definition that there is something that creates the universe.", "The word \"create\", \"bring into existence\", \"cause\", you can use whichever term you want. We are coming to \"the will\" part and \"the intelligent\" part, because this is what is making you not wanting to use the word \"create\". But I'll grant you not using the word \"create\". I am very particular with my terminology, because Atheists are very sensitive. I use that in a particular language to make sure that Atheists don't get triggered by what I'm saying.", "That thing, whichever caused the universe, I did talk about \"knowledge\". \"Intelligent\" is a human term. In the Quran or in the Hadith, what's interesting is one of the Attributes of Allah is \"Knowledge\". \"Intelligence\" is not a word used. It's a word that we can use in the Arabic language; it's not used in that sense as an Attribute for Allah. But knowledge is used. And we need to agree by necessity that it's not just some type of knowledge: knowledge beyond our understanding is necessary for you to cause. You cannot cause: have laws, create a universe, precise measurements, all of these things that happened. You cannot do any of that without a type of knowledge.", "You're not familiar with the idea of the Big Bang, what it is, how it happens, or even any of the laws that govern our universe. If you were familiar with how precise and detailed these things are, that question would even be out of the picture. For example, Martin Rees' book, called \"Just Six Numbers\". He's an atheist! And the only way he tries to escape this intricate detail is to come up with something that has no evidence, which is the idea of the multiverse that has zero evidence; even him would admit to that. Like Stephen Hawking, the one who brought it forward, said I have no evidence for it. But he knows it's so intricate, detailed and specific. We're talking about now zillions of numbers. If you were to change just one decimal, the universe wouldn't exist.", "So knowledge there, and not just knowledge — knowledge is beyond any understanding that we possess today — is a necessity for the universe that we have today to exist. It's just the time that the atoms start to exist, the speed that the universe expands in, the electromagnetic waves, the way of the atom, all of these things. I can go to every single specific detail of the universe, and there's no way anyone would disagree that you would require knowledge to do that cause. Now, that's why we give the attribute \"All-Knowledgeable\" to The Creator. We don't say all intelligent. I don't know who does that, but it's not us as Muslims.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is God Necessary? Intellectual Atheist Challenges The Muslim Understanding Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOPzUt3vmGs", ((657, "10:57–11:32"), (731, "12:11–13:07"), (792, "13:12–13:51"), (878, "14:38–15:17"), (924, "15:24–16:11"),), id: 95)
