#passage(("Now, when it comes to the will: what is a will? A will is a decision between two things. Decision to initiate an action, which will be, by necessity, between one of two options. Because if you can only do one thing, you're forced. So if I force you to do one thing, there's no longer will. So to choose between two things: The Creator, or whatever causes the universe — to not use that language you don't like — had two choices: cause the universe or don't cause the universe. And it chose, through \"will\", to cause the universe. So you just admitted to the existence of \"will\".", "On top of that, to add cherries to the cake, you will apply that to every single thing that exists in the universe. Now, that cause could have resulted in one planet, could have resulted in two planets, could have resulted in zillion planets. Could have resulted in blue planet or yellow! Only blue, only yellow, only red! Could have resulted in dark matter or white matter or blue matter or orange matter. Now, the fact that all of these things are precise necessitates choice, because there's more than one option and it could have been otherwise! Now, the fact that it \"chooses\" is the will. So now, by necessity, that creator requires the will. Otherwise, it cannot cause the universe.", "I love the atheists when they make the argument: something caused the universe! The minute you say something caused the universe, I already have you in my hand now. You already have admitted to everything I need. So even if you don't want to admit that something caused the universe, either way, you're stuck. The Truth is undeniable.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Is God Necessary? Intellectual Atheist Challenges The Muslim Understanding Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOPzUt3vmGs", ((973, "16:13–16:54"), (1022, "17:02–18:23"), (1105, "18:25–18:43"),), id: 97)
