#passage(("You said your sources of knowledge are testimony and empiricism and intuitive knowledge, and you said this is how you believe — and you're a naturalist — you can know about things. Then you jumped on to tell us there are multiple universes that you \"believe\" exist, other than our bubble that we exist in, which contradicts everything you just said about sources of knowledge, because you can never know about them, because you're limited to the universe that you're in. So you have to \"believe\", whether you like the world or not; you have to have blind faith that there are other alternative universes, because you can never see, observe, or bring any evidence for these alternative universes. It's just to try to escape the existence of God.", "Then you add on top of that — because you find an issue now! The universe has a beginning that would require a cause. So you say, OK, I'll believe in a cause, but because you guys believe in God and I don't want to say the word \"God\", I'm going to say this \"cause\" is powerful, knowledgeable, but you know what! He just does things in physical ways and natural ways. He just doesn't do it in other ways. And he doesn't have a will! Do you think as an Atheist that this is a rational position to have? You just contradicted what you started with.", "Then you bring something you say you believe in, which you have no evidences for and you can never have evidences for. It's just like someone says \"I believe in a pink flying elephant somewhere\". It's something we cannot observe. It's invisible and nobody can observe or sense. It's similar to say I believe that there're alternative universes, because you can never observe these things or see them! We haven't even fully discovered the universe that we exist in, let alone say that there are other universes that exist.", "You say it does things in a physical manner or a naturalistic manner or whatever. Why? Where do you bring this necessity from? Why does the Creator need to start the universe in a physical naturalistic way? Why does the eternal thing that has knowledge and has power beyond our imagination, and eternal, and can create universes, why can't it create the universe but in a way not physical and materialistic the way you wanted to say it does?", "What is called natural laws is what we function in in the universe, like laws of gravity and all of these things, which are created by that thing! Let's use the word \"it\". I'm not using the word \"he\" for a man or a woman, because we don't believe God is a man anyway. I'm just using language. So if it created the universe, and gave it the laws that it's in, you somehow are trying to tell us that it is restricted by the same laws \"it\" created. If it creates universes, then by definition, the laws that are in the universe that is created by that thing is given by that thing.", "My main interest when I talk to someone is whether they believe in a Creator or not, because that is where they're going to end up in the Afterlife. The most important thing is whether someone is a believer or disbeliever of God.", "Why would you believe it does things through natural processes? It's just a \"belief\" that you have. But the reason you have that belief is because you don't want to accept the idea of God. You don't want to use the term God. You don't want to accept that there is something that is called God, that brings the universe into existence. But what you're missing is everything you admitted to in our conversation, in the end, leads you, by necessity, to come to that conclusion. You have to believe in a God in the theistic position that we hold: Powerful in a way we do not know, has a will, and creates the universe.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Philosopher Challenges Muslim On The Creation Of The Universe! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zm8zgi2WPO4", ((1105, "18:25–19:11"), (1151, "19:11–19:41"), (1200, "20:00–20:20"), (1236, "20:36–21:06"), (1286, "21:26–22:48")