#passage(("What do you mean by evidence? Because if you assume the only type of evidence is that an individual has to have a one-to-one, actual experience with God, I reject this notion. Evidence does not necessarily mean that I need to see God, touch God, or the five empirical senses, for example, hearing, smelling, touching: empiricism. That's not the only way we can know about something. So it's something called the study of knowledge. It's called epistemology, the sources of knowledge, how you can know about something. There are many various ways to know about something: induction, deduction, testimony.", "I'll give you an example. Have you been to China before? But you believe that China exists. But how do you know that's China? So you trust the testimony of Google maker or the Google software developer that tells you this is China. So that is what we call testimony. You've not been to China, touched it, felt it, or smelled it yourself, but you knew about China through a valid source of information, which is a trustworthy source that gives you testimony.", "Have you walked in the land that is called China and seen the buildings? Or do you just accept that there is a land today called China, and they have all of these manufacturing places that they've manufactured for the rest of the planet? Even though you've not been there, but you do believe that without a shadow of a doubt? You accepted the testimony of your friends; it's also testimony. So when you tell me there are Chinese people that I know, they give you the testimony: they testified that they are Chinese and they've been to China. These pictures, for you to know that they are China, you're trusting who's telling you this is China. Could this picture have been taken in the country you're in, in a place you've not been to? Yes, it's possible. All of this is still considered testimony.", "So when you buy that ticket online, have you touched the ticket? Have you smelled the ticket? So how do you know that you have a ticket now? In this hypothetical situation, you purchased the ticket to go to the so-called country that you're talking about. So when you accept the testimony of the company that told you this is a valid ticket that will work if you go to the airport, that's called testimony. When you go and take the bus or the car and you've not met the driver ever in your life, you trust the testimony of the council or the country that they're going to hire reliable drivers. You don't know the person. You don't know if he can drive, but you go to the airplane, and you could lose your life if he doesn't know how to drive. But you trust that they are going to hire reliable individuals who know how to drive a plane. But you still fly with a plane?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Lady Questions Muslim On Evidence Of God! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nk4EM0u0VNs", ((32, "0:32–1:08"), (82, "1:22–2:07"), (143, "2:23–3:20"), (202, "3:22–5:53"),), id: 182)
