#passage(("I think you're misunderstanding how argumentation works in this case. When I ask you for a historical event, not a religious teaching, you don't find that in the religious sources; you find that in historical sources. So if I ask you when was a specific historical event between two armies that were fighting each other, then, especially if it's related to a prophecy, you would give me a historical source which we can investigate and confirm that the event that you're claiming happened, happened that way. Now, when I tell you the Quran says something, obviously it will exist in the Quran, because that's the only source it would exist in. When I say to you Allah, from a Muslim point of view the God of Islam, said something, that cannot exist in external sources by definition, because the words of Allah exist only in the Quran. Obviously, this is a huge conflation between the two. Now, if I were to ask you, for example, where does your religion say something, then you should find me that in your scripture. I was asking you a historical event.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Mormon Debates Muslim On The Validity Of His Faith! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0dXtlG11q-g", ((1768, "29:28–30:31"),), id: 187)
