#passage(("The beginning of the Quran says something beautiful. So we have chapter:1 (Alfatiha), which is very small, and then we have the biggest chapter, which is chapter:2 (Albaqara2:2-4), which is the real beginning, you can say. (This is the Book about which THERE IS NO DOUBT, a Guidance for those who are conscious of Allah. Those who believe in the unseen, establish prayer, and spend out of what We provided for them. Those who believe in what was revealed to you, and what was revealed before you, and they are of the Hereafter CERTAIN.) So the Quran here is saying a few things. First, this Book has no doubts. So I don't need to go in a circle around myself. Second thing, it says that this book is for those who are certain of the Afterlife. I've not seen the afterlife.", "I accept its existence. I don't like to use the word \"belief\". I'll tell you why. Because in the West, people think belief means to accept something without evidence: \"oh, he just believes it!\" And we don't believe that. We say that belief, in our perspective, is something that is based on evidence as well. But once I reach a sufficient level of evidence, I don't go around myself again as a circle and start questioning it. I accept the assumptions. And no one actually applies philosophy in their life. If you doubt everything, you should doubt whether something can drop on top of you right now from the sky, then you die! So you're not even going to exit your house. You doubt that maybe you catch some diseases from here or there. It's just some idea in the brain that is wasting your time.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Truth About Philosophy! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xoYG4Qi-lx4", ((476, "7:56–8:37"), (523, "8:43–9:24"),), id: 188)
