#passage(("Science explains something which is already in existence. For example, if your auntie bakes a cake, science can explain how the cake was baked: how much sugar, how much salt. But science itself is not a human being that will bring the material together of the cake. You require something to do that. So you require your auntie, and you require the materials to be there. And you require your auntie as a cause to make the cake. Then science just observes something which is already there. So science can observe and explain how the universe functions, but it cannot tell us where the universe came from, or the material of the universe.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The Truth About Philosophy! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xoYG4Qi-lx4", ((173, "2:53–3:30"),), id: 200)
// U ML371:53-157 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("You said the way the world was created in The Bible is nonsense. There's a beautiful verse in the Quran, this verse in the Quran18:51. It says: مَّا أَشْهَدتُّهُمْ خَلْقَ السَّمَاوَاتِ وَالْأَرْضِ (I did not make them -i.e the people- witness the Creation of the Heavens and the Earth). Did you witness how the Creation happened? Or how the Universe started? Were you there at the point of the origin of the Universe? So how can you know for a fact how the Universe started, since, by the way, we cannot know for a fact through the scientific method? So you're assuming that the biblical tradition of Creation is incorrect, while you cannot witness the beginning of the Creation.", "And then you were going to go to the Quran as well, trying to say why the Quranic account of the Creation is also incorrect. Why? You did not witness it yourself; you cannot know how the Universe started. If you cannot know that for a fact, then you cannot make any positive claims whether something is correct or incorrect. You cannot say this was not the way it was created, because you cannot demonstrate that.", "I'm not defending Christianity, and I'm not Christian obviously. But you said that you left Christianity because the account of the Creation — you don't agree with it. Now, if we've established that we cannot know how the Creation started or how the Universe started, that's not a logical point for you to denounce Christianity.", "I'm not disagreeing that you can have probability, that you can look at certain things and you can look at certain evidences and then you can say something is more probable. But I'm not talking about probability; I'm talking about certainty. Because if you make a positive claim, you need to have evidence 100% to back it up, if you want to say 100% it did not happen this way. We're talking about the beginning of the Universe. I'm not talking in general here, I'm talking specifically. So you cannot then neglect a Scripture on the basis that it says the 