// U ML131:125-173 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("Probabilities don't exist in reality. Atheists should never mention probability, because in your worldview, which is a materialistic worldview, there is no probabilities. Give me something that is probable, that is 50%. For example, a coin flip. Is a coin flip probability 50-50%, heads or tails? That's incorrect. That coin flip, we know 100% whether it's going to be heads or tails based on knowing the factors that influence the flip. Meaning, if I know how much force exactly I'm putting into the throw, if I know the gravity, if I know which angle the coin is going to hit, if I know where is it going to land and what else is going to hit when it lands, and when I flipped it, was it heads or tails? I will know exactly whether the coin will end up being heads or tails.", "Now, there is a French scientist who made a machine that flips coins and always gives you the same thing. Why? Because this machine is set with a certain force, certain angle, all of that. So it always 100% guarantees you that you will get that. So that means it's no longer 50-50 possibility. So why do we say it's 50-50 possibility? The reason we say it's 50-50 possibility is because we are ignorant of the external factors. So the word random or probable is just a reflection of the human ignorance of every variable that is at play.", "Same thing with the AI. The AI is programmed, and if I know the exact programming of the AI, I will know whether the programming is going to lead me to this answer or that answer, or this \"random\" list or that \"random\" list, because whatever the AI is going to present to you is based on the input data that you put in the AI. And you programme the AI to do probabilities. So if you completely understand how the AI works, and every variable to do with how the AI works, you will be able to tell exactly what the AI is going to present to you before the AI presents it. So it's not random. You look at the stars, you think they're random; they're not. I'll bring a