// U ML033:373-412 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("I think I'm more open-minded than anybody else, because I've actually looked at Buddhism. Looked at Christianity, looked at Hinduism. I looked at Judaism. I read the books. I'm talking about [Talmud], Tanak. I'm talking about the Vedas. I'm talking about these specific religious scriptures, and engaging with monks. By the way, I have discussions with monks, have discussions with priests, have discussions with rabbis, I have discussions with everybody.", "I think if open-minded means you don't have a set of convictions and beliefs that you reach after research, that's not being open-minded. I think being open-minded is being open enough to engage in discussions with other people. Exchange ideas, explore where you might be wrong, but you apply reasoning to reach who's right and who's wrong. So, that's the definition of open-minded. But not to have a conviction, I don't think is open-minded. I think it means that you stand for nothing, and you can fall for anything if you stand for nothing. You can believe in anything if you stand for nothing.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Buddhist Gets Defensive After A Muslim Highlights The Problems With Buddhism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCyN0gL4kEI", ((1115, "18:35–19:58"), (1200, "20:00–20:31"),), id: 224)
