#passage(("So where do you bring your morality from? Before asking moral questions, what is your yardstick of determining what is right or wrong? Because you're assuming they're wrong if you're asking them, so you should have a yardstick to determine why it's right or wrong.", "You said they're subjective, which means that there's no objective morality, which means that there's no right and wrong; it just depends on the subjective opinion. Do you know what does the word subjective mean? It's based on the subject. So the subject Mac Mac and the subject Muhammed. If the subject Muhammed says eating pork is immoral, the subject Mac Mac says eating pork is moral. The action of eating pork objectively, outside of Muhammed and Mac, there's no determination of whether it's right or wrong, because it's all based on Mac and Muhammed. If we were not there, it would not be neither right or wrong. So subjective by definition means that there is no objective right or wrong. It's just a personal opinion of what you'd think yourself is right and what you'd think yourself is wrong. The action itself outside of you and me is not really right or wrong, because it needs you and me to determine.", "It's irrelevant if there's 1 or 2 or 3, because all what they're going to say to you — the people who believe it's Ok — they will say to you: that's your opinion, I don't need to accept your opinion, it's your subjective opinion. I think it's Ok; you cannot enforce your subjective morality on others because it's not objective. So whether the majority believes in it or not, that still does not change the fact that it's subjective. So I believe the killing innocent people is objectively wrong, but I have an objective standard of morality; you don't.", "But I'm gonna answer you based on what I view as an objective moral view. It's not my opinion; it's what the Quran and Sunnah say. Whether I'm here or not, or whether any Muslim is here or not, the Quran is there still; objectively right and wrong is still there in the Quran. You're bringing your opinion based on your upbringing, so that's the difference between me and you.", "You said you will ask moral questions, so I'm establishing a reality first, is that if you don't have a standard of right or wrong, if you ask these questions based on thinking [they're] wrong, and is just your subjective opinion, I can just say \"Ok, I disagree with your subjective opinion,\" and that can be a basic answer to your question. I don't need to give you reasoning. You don't have something enforceable in order for me to say \"Oh! let me explain this, you're right.\"",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Philosopher Attempts To Challenge A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hGbkF45B_A0", ((15, "0:15–0:20"), (56, "0:56–1:39"), (111, "1:51–2:10"), (147, "2:27–2:47"), (183, "3:03–3:17"),), id: 229)
// U ML411:207-285 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("You're born here, you lived here, your parents taught you. So in the end, what you think will be a product of your surroundings, whether you like it or not, even if you want to tell yourself that \"it was me.\" But it was you picking from the things around you still. You are still picking from the western narrative of morality. And that's a subjective thing. If you were to be in a different country you would believe opposite to what you believe right now! Most likely! On a probabilistic basis, you would. If you were born in China you would eat a dog, no problem; there's no issues.", "So the only reason you believe killing is wrong, raping is wrong, is because your subjective product of your society told you that! And then morality will then also be unenforceable; you cannot enforce it. If Hitler was chopping heads here and you said to him \"This is wrong!\" he would say to you \"To me, it's right!\" You have no right to enforce your subjective morality on another subjective individual.", "Then we say as Muslims \"we do not follow that,\" because that doesn't make any sense and that would only cause chaos in the world, which would mean allow anyone to do whatever they want to do, because there is no right and wrong in reality. But we do believe humans innately believe there's right and wrong, innately believe that there is morality; innately they know deep down inside of them that there is a right and there is a wrong, to an extent! Which we call the Fitra. Fitra is an innate disposition given by the Creator to the creation to recognize certain aspects of life to be right or wrong. Now, Muslims don't believe in subjective morality; they believe in objective morality. Objective is that which is outside of you, your ideas, your desires and your emotions. We believe it comes from the Creator.", "If I made a phone or if I made these headphones, I would know about it more than anyone else, because I'm the manufacturer, the maker. If I tell you \"don't do this with it\" or \"do this with it,\" you would follow what I tell you to do, because you know that I know more about it and I know what's beneficial and harmful for it. If we are created by a Creator, then it follows logically that Creator knows about us the most, so He can tell us our objective right and wrongs, to do and not to do! Based on that, Muslims follow objective morality — which comes from God — and they do not just follow their product of their societal morality or where they come from. And based on that, women wear the Hijab, because it's an objective moral code that comes from God, Who is All-Knowing, All-Wise, Who knows about you more than you know about yourself.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sharia Law is Corruption?! Muhammed Ali - Speaker Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R8lvyRvJ_tg", ((685, "11:25–11:57"), (726, "12:06–13:18"), (809, "13:29–14:16"), (856, "14:16–14:56"),), id: 230)
// U ML156:388-436 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("You struggle to accept that \"Allah says this and that, but then this and that happens.\" The Quran already tells us that we will have suffering in life. So, when you say you struggle to accept that there is suffering in life when Allah told you there will be suffering in life, that makes no logical sense.", "I asked you, \"Where do you bring your ethics from?\" And you told me \"Life experience and society.\" That is the most subjective thing that you could say. So, I'll put you in a time machine now and we'll bring you to three, four thousand years in the past, where slavery was a part of society. Your life experience in your society will tell you that slavery is a very moral thing. Happens on a daily basis, and you will live under that, and based on that worldview, then slavery is OK. It was correct in a specific time. Now it's not. So your morality is relative. It's based on the society, the time, and it changes from day to another.", "But if there is a Creator, which IS the case, then by default, that morality — that comes from the Creator — is objective. So you cannot make the claim, \"Oh, no, religion has this, religion has that.\" No, if that religion is true and that God exists, then by definition, that morality is objective. It's no longer an opinion, like the one you're coming with. So if we operate from an objective worldview where we know for a fact that there is a creator, then that is an objective moral standard, and we're not using a subjective opinion of someone who was born in the 21st century telling us: \"Why it's fair and why it's not fair.\" We're not interested!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Emotional Atheist Receives A Reality Check From A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ha146KFce5w", ((1005, "16:45–17:00"), (1025, "17:05–17:31"), (1071, "17:51–18:22"),), id: 231)
