#passage(("Where do you get your morality from? If it's from the society, then it's moral relativism. It means: number one, is subjective; number two, it changes with time. And that's why you can go to the UK in 1960s or before: they were hanging people from that community in the streets. They were doing that. And then gradually, the law changed after \"sexual revolution,\" \"enlightenment period,\" etc.", "If morality was based on where your community is, every community has its own laws. And what that would mean? It would mean, first, that Muslims are completely right within their laws, because they're societal laws, because all these punishments can only be applied in a Muslim community. So you would be essentially admitting that Muslims, what they're doing, is perfectly moral. Not only that, you also, in a way, are saying that slavery is right when it happened in the past, because that community believed that slavery was right. And you're also saying that punishing the LGBT community and putting them to death was right, because that time the community agreed to it. That is what your position implies.", "If you say from your own self, then we'll tell you, with all due respect: why does anybody need to follow what you think is right or wrong? It's your opinion! It's a subjective personal opinion for an individual in a specific country among 8 billion people. Why do we need to listen to whatever you think?", "The unhappiest countries on earth are the atheistic countries. They have the highest suicidal rates. Because the problem is, whatever studies you're looking at are looking at GDP (Gross Domestic Product) or whatever per capita. They're looking at the money, how much money they're getting. That's not happiness! Look at the studies that show depression, anti-depression meds, suicide and all of that. The first of the list, you will see ALL the atheistic countries: Sweden, Denmark, all of these, number one in the list. Look at Pew Research Institute. When it comes to suicide, anti-depression meds, all of this stuff, in every societal metric the west is failing.", "If you look at Blanchflower and Oswald, there's a study that was done, 100,000 women. It's probably the biggest study that was done. It looks at women happiness from feminism, 1960s or 70s, until today, or recent 2000s. And it is showing this decline of happiness in women among the West. They're all getting anti-depression meds. They're unhappy, all of them. If you look at the Pew Research Institute, it has specific studies where it compares religious people and non-religious people. I can present you so many studies where it shows that the happiest people, when it comes to self-satisfaction, happiness, all of these things, are religious people, according to Pew Research Institute, according to non-Muslim institutes.", "Plus, it's not an argument anyways! We were talking about where right or wrong comes from, not who's happy and who's not. A [rapist] or whatever can be very happy doing what he's doing. Happiness is not a determiner: if you're happy or not happy, then, therefore, what you're doing is right or wrong.", "So now, all the questions that you've been asking me, even though I was giving you rational answers, I could just tell you I disagree with you. And that should be more than sufficient, because I should be entitled to my opinion in your worldview, the way you are entitled to your opinion. So what makes certain people in the West think that their morality is superior, and they get to tell us what to do with our women, or what to do with our houses, or how to deal with our families, since all of their worldviews are subjective?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tran$gender Challenges Muslim On His Comments About LGTBQ Community! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XPGGQKYFYqo", ((1661, "27:41–28:16"), (1708, "28:28–29:17"), (1790, "29:50–30:04"), (1828, "30:28–30:59"), (1863, "31:03–31:45"), (1909, "31:49–32:07"), (1956, "32:36–32:57"),), id: 235)
