#passage(("Normally, if I decide to eat my own baby, is that good or bad? Right now, if you go and beat up an old lady, just walking by, is that action you did bad or good? So, me wiping off half of China because there is not enough space for the other half, that's a good utility; does that make it good? Why is it bad? Why is it stupid? They have a justification, rational justification. They say, we don't have enough space. There are so many human beings. We want to get rid of half of them. So they would argue that it's not as stupid; it's actually genius. So, based on your worldview, why is that action bad?", "These are problems, or let's say rational problems. We're trying to understand whether there's objective or subjective morality, and how do we determine. We care because this is how we function as human beings. Before doing an action, I have to determine. It's not about \"drive.\" It's about understanding rationally as a human being, before doing a specific action, is it good or bad? If I don't have morality, then me pushing you now or me harming this person is equal to me being good to him. I'm actually thinking right now, is it good or bad to have this discussion?", "For me, being human, my instincts are God given by the Creator. They're within me, and they are the ones that drive me to move and make actions. The point I'm trying to make is this: if there is no objective morality, life becomes absurd, which is why I was trying to demonstrate to you that eating your baby in your breakfast, or you have intercourse with your own mother, becomes a normal thing, because there's no right or wrong.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Struggles With Morality! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4SowrflBcT0", ((331, "5:31–7:12"), (444, "7:24–8:37"), (522, "8:42–9:11"),), id: 236)
