#passage(("Can you recognize good without evil? That would mean that we have to have evil in the world by necessity, so we can understand the concept of what good is. So that's the first point I'm going to make. The second point I'm going to make is in most religions, or in Islam — let me talk about Islam because this is our religion — this life is a test. So we are accountable. What God does is He creates us and He gives us this propensity to do evil and to do good, but deep down, we know the main things that are evil. We have it innately; something called the فطرة (Fitra: Natural disposition).", "So I know that killing and raping and stealing — innately — I know it's wrong. I don't know every detail of life in morality, but I know the main things; I have it deep within me. This is a moral compass that The Creator has put within us. So if I go and do evil, I know deep down it's evil. He sends us a Revelation which has all aspects of Morality, not just the things that are within you, and then The Creator is testing you whether you will choose to do that which is good, or you will choose to do that which is evil.", "There's a concept that a lot of people don't take into consideration, which is the idea of the Afterlife, in which everyone is accountable for their deeds. So if I do evil, that doesn't mean that someone is going to get away with it right now. In [the Afterlife], if you do evil you'll be accountable. People will be recompensated for the bad that happened to them and evil people will be punished. So when you look at the whole picture of Islam now, where is the problem?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Gay Student Confronts Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XqXTdyMc3OU", ((20, "0:20–0:56"), (62, "1:02–1:21"), (105, "1:45–2:04"),), id: 310)
