#passage(("First I want to start with a very important correction: the idea \"All-Loving\" is not a term that we give to God. The \"Most Loving\" is how we describe Him. If you imagine \"All-Loving\" to mean that there's nothing... like, \"Everything is going to be butterflies!\" and this and that, then you will have an issue, yes. The issue with the people who view this idea of the Problem of Evil is that they view a creator that religious people don't usually believe in: a Creator abstract of all of His attributes.", "The default position in everything is goodness; it's not evil. Evil is an exception. That's why — for example — you're healthy for almost all your life, and then sickness is something that happens to you not often, here and there. That is not the default position that you live in. Murder is not the default position that people live in. An overwhelming majority of the people are living their life. So, the reality is, we're living in a state of bliss in most situations, and then there is an existence of Evil within that life of goodness.", "Evil is relative. Something might be bad for you but good for me. If I'm sick, sickness is not necessarily the best for me, but it's good for the doctor. The doctor is probably going to get out of business; there's no more business if I don't get sick and you don't get sick. That is good for him, but it is not good for me. That good and bad is relative to the individual and relative to the situation. You have someone going and gambling, losing all his money, but the other person won all his money. So, maybe it's bad for you, you have a terrible day, evil day, but the other person has a fantastic day, he made a lot of money. If you look at the bigger picture, that same evil can be good for someone else.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Asks Muslim To Explain Why God Allows Suffering! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5-bfwhR0lSM", ((107, "1:47–2:26"), (204, "3:24–3:51"), (241, "4:01–4:42"),), id: 317)
