#passage(("A lot of people have this experience, by the way: they have experienced some sort of suffering in their life when they're very deeply religious, and then a lot of them leave. But there's something very interesting there for me. Suffering should be a given when you believe in God. This claim I would personally make, because if you truly believe in a Creator and you claim that you believe in a Creator, a test is the only thing that would prove if you truly believe in a Creator or not.", "God has put us in this life. From our perspective as Muslims, we believe we're here to be tested. This life is a test. If this life is a test then there will be trials and tests happening in this life, and God rewards those who do right and then he punishes those who do evil. So because we are living in a test — this is the purpose of our existence in this life — then we'll encounter these sort of things.", "But you claim — this is the thing — you chose to claim you are a believer in that situation or scenario. So if someone chooses to claim that he's a believer, then shouldn't it be a given, if a test comes, that he shows that true belief that he has in his heart and manifest it by sticking on his faith?", "Because if you have a friend, and then a friend says, I love you as a friend and all of that, when you are in need — you need money, you need help — you call him, he doesn't respond, he doesn't help you, then you would know he doesn't truly love you. Because it's not about the statement that you make, the claim, but it's about the behaviour that you take afterwards. So we say God test us in this life. Because anyone can claim, oh God, I believe in you. Okay, you believe in me, but when the difficult test happens, what do you do?", "And sometimes we do love worldly things more than we love God. It happens. There are people who love their children, their wives, the money, and God tests us with these things: okay, you believe in me? Okay, I'm going to take away that thing that you claim you love me more than. So let's see, are you actually going to behave in accordance to what you say or not. So this is a part of why we exist. That's what we believe as Muslims: we're not here in this life to play around and have fun. We believe that's in afterlife.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Presents Muslim With Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NtXZBPaQ2NQ", ((29, "0:29–1:03"), (72, "1:12–1:33"), (93, "1:33–1:54"), (114, "1:54–2:37"), (157, "2:37–3:04"),), id: 321)
// U ML366:58-132 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("I think the happiest people today are people who have the least amount of materialistic needs. In the West today, it is the highest level of depression ever. They're taking pills, they don't know their own gender. People are confused. Go to Africa in a village. Look at how happy the people are. You will see the true level of happiness there. Least amount of materialism, least amount of technology and this and that. They live an actual life, interaction with human beings, eating from the natural food that Allah has given them, the water, all of that. They are the happiest people on earth.", "Do these people become atheists or do they believe more in God? The argument that's usually being made: why don't they say, okay, there's all these sufferings, it's impossible for God to know that I'm a Muslim and then let me suffer this way? This is the more rational thing to say if it's rationality. So, what I'm saying is, because they know clearly, stipulated in the Quran, this life is a test. So, in order for me to be tested, I have to experience good and bad. It cannot be a test unless you've experienced both. So, the person that — let's say — murders another person: if Allah were to stop him from murdering, not allow him to murder at all, Allah cannot test that person without allowing him to do evil to begin with.", "But what people do is, they take away the idea of the afterlife. Those people know that, because they know in the afterlife they will be recompensated for what is happening. They know, if they were to reject God, knowing deep down that there has to be a Creator, all what's going to happen is, they suffer in this life, they're also suffering in the afterlife. For whatever happens to them in this life, there will be a compensation in the Afterlife, because they know Islam is the Truth. We can prove it.", "So, it's not like we just believe that Quran and Islam is the truth. No, there are evidences that show that Islam and Quran is The Truth. If I'm a rational human being, do you think I'm going to be doing everything I'm doing without evidences that this is The Truth? If I do not have evidences, rational logical evidences, that this is from God — I know for certain it is — would I be standing here in this weather, -3° today, talking to you about this and that? I would be home, there's a heater.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Questions Muslim On The Existence Of Evil! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ov3dkTJn6s", ((161, "2:41–3:21"), (222, "3:42–4:44"), (288, "4:48–5:19"), (325, "5:25–5:56"),), id: 322)
