#passage(("When I look at someone else who doesn't have something and who's suffering, I appreciate the health that God has given me, and I start thanking God for that which He's given me that He's not given to the other individual. It also gives me an opportunity, a test: am I going to help that person or not? The only way you can recognize good is with the existence of evil. If everything was good, you wouldn't know what evil is, and if everything was evil, you wouldn't know what good is. They balance one another and they are necessary in this world of testing. That is why we have the Afterlife, in which everything in the Afterlife is balanced out: everyone who did evil will be punished for the evil they did, and those who did good will be rewarded for the things that did.", "The idea that you have this suffering — for us as Believers — we perceive it differently. I might suffer. Like the wife of the Prophetﷺ said, even if the small needle hits you, it removes, expiates from your sin. The suffering that you can have in this life can remove the sins, because you're living, you're doing evil things. It can elevate your status in the afterlife; you can have a better place in Paradise because you lived in that life. So, evil and suffering, it's relative. It doesn't necessarily mean it's bad for everyone, as you perceive it. You don't understand the whole picture of this life. This life is a test; that's why when you perceive something, it might be the opposite of how you perceive.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Asks Muslim To Explain Why God Allows Suffering! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5-bfwhR0lSM", ((469, "7:49–8:40"), (523, "8:43–9:20"),), id: 323)
// U ML145:85-130 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("You have choices every day in your lifetime. The test is your lifetime, and the decisions you have are what God commands you to do, which would be the good things, morally good, and what God forbids you from doing, which are going to be the evil deeds, which are the path of Satan and your desires. Now, you exist in this life to be tested from the moment you're born until the moment you die. Now, you're not accountable for what you do when you're young, until you reach puberty. When you reach puberty, then you are now accountable for the things that you do. So from that point until your death, what you do and the actions that you make, which is completely your choice, whether it is to obey God or to disobey God, are going to determine your outcome in the Afterlife.", "And if you commit sins and do evil, you are allowed to repent: ask God for forgiveness, and then get forgiveness from The Creator, from God, through your repentance and through stopping the action and giving the rights back to the people and have no intention of coming back to that evil sin again.", "I wouldn't word it in a way which recommends people to do bad so they can learn. No. They say, \"Smart people learn from their mistakes. Wise people learn from other people's mistakes.\" You don't need to do something wrong in order for you to realise that it was wrong. You can look at the outcomes of that thing, and you can follow the guidance of The Creator. But the reason I say that would be bad, because no human being is perfect. So sooner or later, your desires will make you do something which is wrong. So God does not ask you to be perfect. He asks you to do your best. So you fight yourself, you fight against your desires, and if you fall, you try to rectify yourself and come back. And yes, you learn from your mistakes. But that doesn't mean we encourage people to do bad so they can learn that something is wrong.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Liberal Woman Debates Muslim On The Need For Religion! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kBJxu5UYcy4", ((294, "4:54–5:37"), (346, "5:46–6:01"), (392, "6:32–7:17"),), id: 324)
