// U ML335:631-651 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("Perhaps there are certain medications you're going to be given that are going to give you pain, but then they're helping your body. You are feeling suffering because your body is adapting and your immune system is adapting to the medication that you took. I can suffer in order for me to actually appreciate what I get. When you work so hard, then you get something, it's the only time you appreciate it. So, this idea that all suffering is bad is not correct, and that life is just about pleasure. A life with only pleasure is probably a depressing life. Having certain struggles and after the struggles, you reach eternal happiness. That's fine, because you have already been through the struggle. So then going to Paradise is good because you've already been through struggles, so you know the opposite of it. But just being in eternal bliss all the time, you will not appreciate anything.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tunisian Girl Questions Muslim On Women's Rights! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l68vK6nDhbA", ((1813, "30:13–30:50"),), id: 338)
// U ML169:339-384 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("Important behind the suffering is the intention: what are you patient behind the suffering? Because you believe in a Creator. Talking about faith: the disbeliever has no faith, he has no meaning, and that's why you have depression, because: why is this happening to me? There's no explanation. They blame God, by the way; atheist blame God even though they're atheist, because they have to find something to blame or someone to blame for the suffering. Because if you take God out of the equation, it's all materialistic life, then there's nothing to blame. What are you going to blame? Life is just life. So even the atheists fall into that, which they have to blame the Creator even though they don't believe in God.", "Suffering is the most common question, by the way, when it comes to atheism and atheists asking things. It's the most common thing, always being asked that question, because everyone is being tested, so everyone is having some sort of suffering, so everyone has to always seek a meaning behind suffering. And that's why you will see that happiest people are usually religious people, because they can put meaning behind suffering. There's a meaning behind The Suffering that happens: this is happening because life is a test, this is happening because X. So because we can put a meaning behind The Suffering, you'd have religious people being the happiest, less depressed, Etc. You always have [atheists] having a lot more depression out of the uncertainty and the nihilism of life: no explanation, no meaning.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Chinese Revert Shares His Conversion Story To Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7yAJVFrfEBk", ((876, "14:36–15:14"), (914, "15:14–16:05"),), id: 339)
// U ML125:503-535 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("When you talk about a baby and a child in Islam, that baby and a child goes to paradise, and therefore in Islam there's a tradition: prophet told us in the day of judgment the person who suffered the most in this life, from beginning of time to end of time, he will be brought and god will dip him into paradise. Put him in paradise. He'll dip him for a very short amount of time, like you dip something, and God will take him out. God will ask him, \"Have you seen any suffering?\" Now imagine the person who suffered the most in this life. Imagine everything: cancer, losing your family, losing your hair, losing your limbs. Imagine everything could possibly happen to you. And he will say: by you, I have never seen any suffering. Do you know why? Because the overwhelming amount of pleasure and happiness that he experienced in that moment in paradise was enough for him to forget everything.", "This is how we are as human beings. You probably would remember certain times in your life when you suffered more than anything. Now you can even recollect these times. You forgot. It's how we are as human beings. So those babies and infants, they might die for 1 minute, 1 second, whatever that amount of time is. But if the exchange is eternal paradise, eternal never ending happiness, then the person who is suffering really is the person who does harm to them. Because that person who's doing evil to them, he will end up in eternal hellfire. No matter how many years you live, how many years you going to live? Let's say 115 years, the last oldest person I've seen. Let's say you're going to live 116 years. Every day you live past let's say 70 is a torture anyways. Your body doesn't function properly. It's a torture. It's misery. And then when you die, now compare 115 years to infinity. Doesn't matter. It's nothing.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Ladies Discover The True Identity Of Jesus! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ytiIPe7Dmho", ((1352, "22:32–23:17"), (1397, "23:17–24:17"),), id: 340)
// U ML149:161-246 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("What's important right now is that we reflect. Why did God put us in this life? He's put us to test us. How can we follow the command of God in our life? There's commandments of prayer that God has told us. There's commandments of being good to people around us. Commandments of recognizing him as our creator. No other human beings. Nothing human is worthy of worship. So only Allah the creator is the one who's worthy of worship. And if we live our life on that basis, I guarantee you will have satisfaction in your life.", "And when calamities happen, you'll understand. Allah says in the Quran, those who have a calamity, they say we belong to Allah and to Allah we shall return. When we ever have a big calamity, we say these words. Why? We belong to Allah. Meaning Allah has created us. I'm just the creation of God and to him we shall return. Whatever I feel right now, I return to him. My family return to him. If I lost money, I'm going to return to God. And then there either be eternal bless or eternal punishment. So if I lived a good life and I didn't care about the calamity, I'll see God and I will live this life of eternal bless in the end. So these two words of I belong to Allah and to him I shall return gives me complete satisfaction in my life.", "That is a pillar in our faith. We have six pillars of faith. That's one of them. Destiny: things are destined. They happen. And you understand that God is a part of everything that happens in your life. He's put you here and he gives you opportunities and chances. He puts things in your life. So this is all found in the Quran. Allah has already given us all the answers. And then the prophet told us the people, the more righteous they are, the more they're tested. So sometimes some people look: oh, this person prays every day, but he has cancer or he has this sickness. But he's tested more because his faith is more than yours. So he's able to bear more than you because your faith is weak. The first thing is going to shake you. But they have a strong faith. So even if they have a strong test, they still are able to maintain their faith.", "They had their faith. That's why I'm telling you that faith is a foundation that can crash everything. If you talk to them that time, they'll tell you that. They tell you we have God. We understand. We don't cry, because he understands, and not only he understands, he knows that it's going to happen, that there is an afterlife and there is a reward for the things that happens in our life.", "And that's why Allah says in the Quran: do not think those who were killed in the path of God, do not think they're dead; they are alive with their lord, they are happy and waiting for those who will come after them. That is the mentality we have. I don't think that someone passed away, this is bad, this is evil. They lived the righteous life, they did as much as they can, they did good. Allah sees what everyone does. And he rewards us. He's loving and merciful. He created them and he allowed them to be a part of our life and he allowed them to have all of these good moments that we had with them. So read the Quran. Read the Quran and you will see the comfort that I'm telling you, the sources from what God has told me. It doesn't come from anything else.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “English Lady Asks Muslim Why God Allows Human Suffering! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NwyXsinRrF8", ((499, "8:19–8:46"), (526, "8:46–9:28"), (568, "9:28–10:34"), (644, "10:44–11:10"), (670, "11:10–11:53"),), id: 341)
// U ML044:367-392 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("For pain you can take painkillers and stuff like that. But Islam doesn't give you that permission to take your life, because this life is God-given to you. And this life is a test. Is anybody not suffering? By the way, you look at the richest man in the world, you think he's not suffering? He's probably suffering a lot more than you, just behind closed doors. He's got his own issues. If I have a lot of money, everyone want to use me for money. Everyone will get to know me for money. Every woman wants you for your money. Nobody sincerely wants you. You have all of these problems with business and taxes and all that. Thing is, everybody's suffering in some way. And this is our test in this life, and we got to deal with our test as much as we can.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Questions A Muslim On The Number Of Wives Allowed In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=W43s5l4eKag", ((1052, "17:32–18:09"),), id: 342)
