#passage(("There is one true idea that is accepted, but there are heretic groups, but they were mostly early on in Islam. But there are still some Heretics who believe in heretic beliefs. So in Islam, if you read the Quran, it's quite simple that the servant of God, or the person, has a choice that he makes (Ashura42:30). And in the same time, God knows everything. He knows the future, and everything is under Allah's will (Almujadila58:7). These two things are explicitly mentioned in the Quran. This means there is a Compatibilist position to understand these verses. If you take one and leave the other, then you're rejecting clear-cut verses of the Quran, which is a heretic position by default.", "There were early groups who tried to do that, and the Companions of the Prophetﷺ for example completely said \"We're free from these people\". These people are not even Muslims, because they've denied one of the Articles of Faith, which is قدر (Qadar: Destiny). There are 6 articles of Faith; you can not deny any of them, or you're not even a Muslim. You have to believe in all of them. So this idea of Free Will and Determinism, it's usually a very binary thing. Either it's very easy for people to understand and comprehend, or it is difficult, in which then they would have to submit to the concept without getting a specific rational explanation that is given to them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “American Questions Muslim On Free Will In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6uEKa9ZO9u4", ((265, "4:25–4:55"), (301, "5:01–5:32"),), id: 359)
