#passage(("Destiny has 4 stages in Islam; we refer to as قدر (Qadar: Predestination or Destiny), or whatever term that you wanna use for it. The first stage is the Knowledge: Allahﷻ knows everything, so Allah knows the past, Allah knows the future, Allah knows the present, and Allah knows the hypothetical events that didn't even occur; if they occurred, how they would be in reality. So there are certain things that we don't do, but hypothetically even if we did, Allah knows what the situation will be if we did them. So let's say I didn't drink water right now, but Allah knows if I were to drink water what exactly will happen. So this is His extent of knowledge, so Allah knows everything all the time. Also that Knowledge is Eternal; it's not like you'll see in different religions God forgetting one thing and losing his Divinity. There's no such thing. Allah is always Knowing all the time, He knows everything all the time; there's no time where ignorance can seep into the Creator. Allah says (Taha20:52) لَّا یَضِلُّ رَبِّی وَلَا یَنسَى (My Lord, Allahﷻ, He does not make mistakes, He does not forget). He does not get misguided; these are not attributes of God. So that's the first stage.", "Second stage is Writing. So we believe in something that we call اللوح المحفوظ (Allawh Almahfudh); it's called The Preserved Tablet, and it is above, it's not here on Earth, and it is not something that we can reach with our hands ourselves. That Preserved Tablet has every information from the beginning of Creation till the end of Creation. So everything that was to take place, since Allah knows everything, that \"Everything\" that is gonna happen to us and happened from the beginning of the Creation till the end of the Creation is written in The Preserved Tablet. That knowledge is just written down in a text form. That's the second level.", "Also the third level is the Creation. That Allah created everything. Allah created us. Allah created the things that we do. Allah created everything. So everything is created by Allah; there is nothing that is not created by Allah. Allahﷻ says (Azzumar39:62) ٱللَّهُ خَـٰلِقُ كُلِّ شَیۡءࣲۖ (Allah is the Creator of everything). Heavens and the Earth, the mountains, everything like this is created by Allah.", "And the fourth level is the Will. That everything happens under Allah's Will. Since the Creator is All-Powerful, All-Knowing, there cannot be something happening in His Dominion that is not in accordance to His Will. Therefore the Creator would not be All-Powerful. If I'm living under your dominion, and I'm doing things that are against your will, and it is not your will that these things are happening, then by definition you're not an all-knowledgeable, all-powerful creator, because you're not able to stop me from doing things against your will. I'm doing things that oppose your will. So everything that happens in the Dominion of Allah happens under Allah's Will, and these are the four stages of Qadar. So believing in these four things is an obligation on every Muslim; you have to believe in these four stages.", "The outcome is based on the choice that you will make. So that's something you need to understand, because people nowadays, they look at it from the opposite end. That's not how you should look at it. The reason the outcome is there is because you made a choice. So the reason Allah has already written the outcome, He wrote it based on the choices that you will choose to make. You will choose one thing. If you will choose one thing, then there is one outcome for you. So these choices that you will make in the future, Allah knows what choices you will make, and He wrote the choices that you will make. So it is not that you're doing them because they're written. They're written because you will choose them. It's not because Allah wrote it, you're forced to do these specific things. That's not how it works.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Popular Youtuber Asks Muslim Difficult Questions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S7bA8bsNs20", ((298, "4:58–5:58"), (362, "6:02–6:26"), (391, "6:31–6:46"), (410, "6:50–7:20"), (451, "7:31–8:21"),), id: 360)
