#passage(("We don't say things are done by Allahﷻ. Allahﷻ doesn't force anything on us; He doesn't do it on our behalf. So I disagree with that kind of terminology. We choose to do them. And based on that, we're accountable for them.", "It depends on what you mean by predetermination. It's just a terminology which in that way it's not used in the Quran or Hadith anyways; that's a foreign terminology, more of a philosophical terminology. Part of His will, as He said to us, He's given us a choice in this life to choose that which deals with our salvation. We can do certain things. If we do good, we'll be rewarded. If we do bad, we'll be punished (alisra17:18-19). And that is our choice to make. And Allahﷻ doesn't force anything on us, and allows guidance for us, and perhaps puts things in our way, in our path, that we can see the Truth, and then we can choose either to follow it or not.", "If Allah said \"you will become\", meaning he forces you \"to become\" or do specific things, this life would be pointless. How can it be a test when you don't have a choice in what you're going to do? And how would Allahﷻ command you to do good, avoid evil? Don't choose this, choose that. If Allah is telling you these things, then He means that you have a choice to avoid these specific things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Enquires About Qur'an Contradictions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0shFTfDDiro", ((437, "7:17–7:47"), (486, "8:06–9:31"), (581, "9:41–10:03"),), id: 362)
