#passage(("Can you change what Allah has [willed]? The answer is no. You know that there is a God, that Creator. Do you believe the actions that you do are determined, or do you believe that you're choosing to do? The Creator create the universe, and is there cause and effect? Then the behavior you do is based on the previous chain of events that took place, purely based on causality. There's a cause and effect, and the effect takes place based on the cause. The actions you do are based on your previous experiences, the chain of cause and effects that starts with creating the universe. The reason you pick the Quran or not is based on your previous experience, where you grew up, what information you picked up from [society], and that is based in where you were born, and you were born based on where human beings existed; where human beings is based on how God created everything, is based on God initiating everything. You have the ability to choose, but the choices you make are based on the previous things, which means they are shaped by that. So you think you're choosing, but it's just based on your previous experiences.", "The idea is if this cause an effect, then you end up with the world view of determinism. The reason Muslims say it's not determined is because the Creator is the one who affects the choice, gives you the effect of choice. Now this external thing, which is the Creator, God, Allah, gives us the ability to choose. Now regardless of the fact that there's cause and effect, which is irrelevant, which is a fact in reality, Allah gives us the ability to choose. Now where is the contradiction there?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Finds Logical Problems With Islam?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CJuAdZhJIzo", ((729, "12:09–14:44"), (917, "15:17–15:45"),), id: 363)
