#passage(("There's an assumption in philosophy that the human mind is equal to God. You assume that you can understand everything. I'm saying it's an assumption that through your mind you can understand everything. Our mind has a limit just like our eyesight has a limit, just like our hearing has a limit. Why do you assume that the mind is that thing which can understand everything through rational ABCD? The mind has a limit as well.", "You know you have a choice to choose. There is a difference between the theoretical sophistry — if I would call it sophistry — to do with Qada' and Qadar, and reality. In reality you know you're choosing. Whoever tells you, whatever tells you, intuitively you know you're talking to me right now by choice; you know you can click this or that, I can click remove. You know it, so it's irrelevant what people tell you. I don't use the term Free Will, because it's not really free; there are chances that Allah puts in front of you. You didn't choose where you're going to be born, how you going to look, which region you're going to; all of these things can have influences. But still is your choice in the end in the matter. That's why everyone's test is different; your test is different than my test, than everyone else's test. But in the end it's nothing you cannot bear and nothing unjust. And that's the interesting thing: لَا يُكَلِّفُ اللَّهُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا وُسْعَهَا (Allah does not burden any soul with more than it can bear), the end of chapter 2 of the Quran (2:286), Surat Al-Baqara.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Speaks The Truth About The Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uxGJUgMQQSI", ((497, "8:17–8:47"), (548, "9:08–11:02"),), id: 365)
