#passage(("I never said \"Free Will\"; I said \"will\", because our will is always bound to Allah's Will. So it's not \"free\", from an Islamic perspective. So to say your will is free is something that is incorrect, because you're not an independent and all-powerful being. Your will is limited to whether Allahﷻ is OK with that choice you're making or not. But we choose; when I say it's not free, that does not mean that we are forced to choose what we choose. We don't separate; we say it's our choice and it's Allah's Will as well that we're choosing that thing. It's something that a lot of people refer to as \"Compatibilism\".", "You've got libertarianism, which complete free from God: God has no control over you; somehow He's All-Powerful but he cannot control you. It's ridiculous. Christians are different camps as well. You've got the Calvinists. So those libertarian ones are completely going against their Scripture, completely nonsensical point of view. Similarly the other ones are also going against the Scripture and would not have a rational justification to say that everything is forced. If everything is forced, then what's the point of Hellfire and Paradise and all of that? How can God punish you for it?", "We've got a middle ground as Muslims. Allah says in the (Quran81:28-29) لِمَن شَاءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَسْتَقِيمَ (For whoever wills among you to be steadfast, to be good), and the Allah says: وَمَا تَشَاءُونَ إِلَّا أَن يَشَاءَ اللَّهُ رَبُّ الْعَالَمِينَ (And you do not will except that Allahﷻ Wills). So your will always be under Allah's Will; you can not go against the Will of Allah, but Allah affirms for you that you choose: لِمَن شَاءَ مِنكُمْ أَن يَسْتَقِيمَ (For whoever of you wills to choose to be steadfast). So you are choosing to be steadfast, but that choice of yours does not go against God's choices as well. Our position is a compatibilist position; it might appear to certain individuals to be contradictory, but it is not contradictory, because you can choose. And a lot of people have given examples for that. You can choose something and at the same time it is God's Will, and that does not take away from you making that choice. This is our position.", "And I would say this is not an important issue to be thinking about. What is important to be thinking about is whether there is a Creator and that religion is true or not. Because if we establish there is a Creator and we establish that religion is true, that means by necessity, whatever the Scripture says is True. If the Scripture says I'm forced, then I'm forced. If the Scripture says I'm not forced, then I'm not forced. So I would say it's not a fundamental question to ask when it comes to discovering religions and things like that. It's more of a curiosity of knowledge, if you want to get into that kind of debate and discussion. It's a loophole that you're not going to find that answer for, because it is an issue that they don't have an answer for.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Irrational Criteria For God's Existence! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xz7nptUD9Ug", ((1157, "19:17–20:28"), (1237, "20:37–21:13"), (1277, "21:17–22:31"), (1353, "22:33–23:06"),), id: 367)
