#passage(("He says, if Allah knows everything, if Allah knows our choices, then how do we have free will? Allah knowing our choices does not negate the fact that they're still our choices. Very simple: you just self-refuted yourself. \"If Allah knows our choices.\" So what Allah knows is what we will choose. Does He force us to choose it? No! Does He give us the choice to choose between these two things, obedience or disobedience, if we want to? Yes! Does He influence our choice by making us choose one or the other? No! Allah knows the choices that we will make, the choices that we will independently choose.", "When you say we're acting out a script: we do not know what's going to happen in the future, which means therefore at this very moment, when I'm deciding to obey or disobey, it's absolutely my choice, because I'm not God. It's irrelevant where another entity, in this case God, knows or doesn't know, because it's not God that is making the choices, it's me. So if me, the person who's making the choices, do not know what the outcome is, then I'm freely choosing to obey or disobey. And therefore, since I'm choosing to obey or disobey, regardless of Allah knows or He doesn't know, I'm the one making the choice. And therefore, I'm punished for my choice.", "And then he says in Heaven, if there is no evil, then evil is not necessary for choice. Who said evil is necessary for choice? Who made this argument? We say evil is necessary for a test in this life. Because if we can only do good, then where's the test? Where's the punishment and reward? Evil is necessary for the test, not for choice. So you are refuting an argument that we don't even make. It's just a straw man argument.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| 5 Reasons I Left Islam?! Hijabi Says Christians Will Go To Heaven?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wIhGWmIQTJY", ((1946, "32:26–33:06"), (1993, "33:13–33:48"), (2030, "33:50–34:12"),), id: 370)
