#passage(("This is something that some people find problematic and some people don't find problematic: the idea of \"Free Will\", if you want to call it \"Free Will\". Even though we don't believe in free will, but the will to choose. You actually have a choice in what you do. Because there's no absolute free will. You didn't choose which body you're going to be born in, which language you're going to speak; all of these things are determined in a way.", "But the thing is this: as Muslims, there are two types of people who grapple with this: some people who find it very problematic, some people who completely understand it straight away. And I always find that with the people who don't understand it, they never get it, and there's not enough rationality you can give them for them to understand this point. Therefore this is something that those people, in the end, have to accept, because the fact is, this is not a foundation of any of the religions, because this concept you can grapple with in any religion that you want to believe in.", "If there is a Creator, The Creator has to be All-Knowing, has to be All-Powerful, and then therefore He knows what's happening. He knows the future, He knows the past. It's not something that is Muslim-specific or Christian-specific. That's why you will find Calvinists in the Christian tradition who believe one thing, and then you'll find three schools of thoughts there. You find three schools of thoughts in Philosophy: people who are Determinists, people who are completely Free Will, Libertarian, and people in the middle who are Compatibilists. You'll find these schools of thought really in any tradition or any religion, I would say.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “American Questions Muslim On Free Will In Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6uEKa9ZO9u4", ((177, "2:57–3:15"), (199, "3:19–3:40"), (224, "3:44–4:05"),), id: 373)

