#passage(("The question I would ask is where does the Quran say we don't have free will? I agree that it does say things are predestined, that's true, but does it say you don't have a will to choose? Which is the assumption of your question. I want to give you a verse to read so you understand a bit more. Chapter 16 of the Quran, verse 106. It says (Quran16:106) مَن كَفَرَ بِاللَّهِ مِن بَعْدِ إِيمَانِهِ إِلَّا مَنْ أُكْرِهَ وَقَلْبُهُ مُطْمَئِنٌّ بِالْإِيمَانِ (Whoever disbelieves in Allah after their belief, not those who are forced..) So it goes on to talk about someone who disbelieves in Allah and the exception of someone who's forced to disbelieve in Allah.", "In the next verse it says what? (Quran16:107): ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمُ اسْتَحَبُّوا الْحَيَاةَ الدُّنْيَا عَلَى الْآخِرَةِ وَأَنَّ اللَّهَ لَا يَهْدِي الْقَوْمَ الْكَافِرِينَ (This is because they prefer the life of the world over the hereafter. Surely Allah never guides those who CHOOSE to disbelief).", "So this verse here is talking about guidance, هداية, which is what you say is the predestined and all of that. Allah says: Allah does not guide those who اسْتَحَبُّوا الْحَيَاةَ الدُّنْيَا عَلَى الْآخِرَةِ (prefer the life of the world over the hereafter). The action in this verse, is it attributed to Allah or attributed to the people? Allah attributes the استحباب, which is loving this worldly life over the afterlife, to the people. So Allah says that this is a decision they made. If it was that Allahﷻ forced them to do that, it would not make any sense for Allah to attribute the action to them, because they did not choose it. So the fact that Allah attributes it to them and then He says He doesn't guide the disbelieving people is the reason He doesn't guide the disbelieving people: because they chose disbelief by loving this worldly life over the afterlife.", "And then, when you go to the next verse, clarifies even more. It says (Quran16:108): أُولَٰئِكَ الَّذِينَ طَبَعَ اللَّهُ عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمْ وَسَمْعِهِمْ وَأَبْصَارِهِمْ ۖ وَأُولَٰئِكَ هُمُ الْغَافِلُونَ (They are the ones whose hearts ears and eyes are sealed by Allah, and it’s they who are ‘truly’ heedless). The ones that choose this life over the Afterlife are the ones that Allah sealed their heart and their eyes. Allahﷻ does not misguide them until they choose misguidance first.", "If you go to, for example, Surah As-Saff (The Row chapter), verse 5, Allahﷻ says in the (Quran61:5) Moses عليه السلام (peace be upon him) said this to his people: “oh my people why do you harm me and you know I'm the messenger of God to you?” So first he's establishing there that they know that he is the Messenger of God; it's not like they don't know who that person is. So they are going against them even though they know he's the Messenger of God, and then Allah says: فَلَمَّا زَاغُوا أَزَاغَ اللَّهُ قُلُوبَهُمْ (when they chose misguidance, when they deviated, Allah deviated their hearts).", "So Allah's action of misguidance or predestination always follows the choice of the individual. So they're linked. If you choose misguidance then Allah will misguide you; if you choose this life over the Afterlife then Allah will seal your heart. Allah does not misguide someone who does not deserve to be misguided, because Allah says in the Quran Chapter 4 (AnNisa') verse 40: إِنَّ اللَّهَ لَا يَظْلِمُ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍۖ (Allah does not do Injustice the weight of an atom).", "So, this is the answer to the question that you make. You had an assumption that Allah forces people to be misguided. The Quran goes against your assumption, because he clearly says that Allah gives the people a chance, and they choose misguidance, and after they choose misguidance Allah seals their heart.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Tunisian Girl Questions Muslim On Women's Rights! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l68vK6nDhbA", ((71, "1:11–2:56"), (182, "3:02–3:03"), (187, "3:07–3:49"), (234, "3:54–4:10"), (259, "4:19–4:43"), (290, "4:50–5:14"), (319, "5:19–5:29"),), id: 374)
